IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `sea-employee`

-- Seafarers Rehabilitation and Compensation Act 1992 -- worked cases, from the seat of the injured seafarer.
--
-- Every assertion is a claim about the ENCODING on stated facts, not about the law. Expected values were written by hand
-- from the deposited text (C2025C00116), never copied from the encoding's output. No scenario life raised this Act;
-- every case is HYPOTHETICAL.
--
-- Mia (HYPOTHETICAL), an integrated rating employed by an Australian shipping company on a tanker trading between
-- Fremantle and Melbourne, slips on deck on 1 March 2026 and injures her shoulder. Her normal weekly earnings are $2,000
-- and her normal hours 38. She has a dependent spouse and two dependent children. Indexed figures are taken as the
-- compilation prints them; AWOTE is taken as $2,000, an assumed figure.

§ `cases`

§§ `ss 4, 19, 20, 20A`

#ASSERT `Does the Act cover my employment` `a seafarer employed on a prescribed ship, on the ship's business` `interstate, or within or between Territories` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` TRUE
-- A ship trading within one State: covered only through the employer being a trading, financial or foreign corporation.
#ASSERT `Does the Act cover my employment` `a seafarer employed on a prescribed ship, on the ship's business` `within one State only` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` TRUE
#ASSERT `Does the Act cover my employment` `a seafarer employed on a prescribed ship, on the ship's business` `within one State only` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered` `employment the Seafarers Act does not reach is under another workers compensation law, which is not encoded (ss 19, 20, 20A)`
-- On the Australian International Shipping Register; a Comcare employee; an exempt employment: not this Act.
#ASSERT `Does the Act cover my employment` `a seafarer employed on a prescribed ship, on the ship's business` `overseas, or between two places outside Australia` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered` `employment the Seafarers Act does not reach is under another workers compensation law, which is not encoded (ss 19, 20, 20A)`
#ASSERT `Does the Act cover my employment` `a seafarer employed on a prescribed ship, on the ship's business` `interstate, or within or between Territories` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `not covered` `employment the Seafarers Act does not reach is under another workers compensation law, which is not encoded (ss 19, 20, 20A)`
#ASSERT `Does the Act cover my employment` `a seafarer employed on a prescribed ship, on the ship's business` `interstate, or within or between Territories` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `not covered` `employment the Seafarers Act does not reach is under another workers compensation law, which is not encoded (ss 19, 20, 20A)`
#ASSERT `Does the Act cover my employment` `a company or industry trainee` `coastal trading under a general licence, or an emergency licence on the Australian General Shipping Register` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` TRUE
#ASSERT `Does the Act cover my employment` `something else` `interstate, or within or between Territories` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered` `employment the Seafarers Act does not reach is under another workers compensation law, which is not encoded (ss 19, 20, 20A)`

§§ `ss 3, 9`

-- The arguments: what was suffered; material contribution (disease); where; otherwise arose; abnormal risk; risky route;
-- discipline or a missed promotion.
-- Mia's fall on deck: an injury (s 9(2)(b)(i)).
#ASSERT `Is it an injury under the Seafarers Act` `a physical or mental injury, other than a disease, or an aggravation of one` FALSE `on board the ship I was employed on` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: it is an "injury" under s 3`
-- Hurt in a car crash driving home from the port after signing off: an injury (s 9(2)(e)(i)) -- unlike the SRC Act.
#ASSERT `Is it an injury under the Seafarers Act` `a physical or mental injury, other than a disease, or an aggravation of one` FALSE `travelling between home and work, or another journey listed in s 9(2)(e)` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: it is an "injury" under s 3`
-- The same, but she detoured to a pub and drove on: the interruption substantially increased the risk (s 9(3)).
#ASSERT `Is it an injury under the Seafarers Act` `a physical or mental injury, other than a disease, or an aggravation of one` FALSE `travelling between home and work, or another journey listed in s 9(2)(e)` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: it did not arise out of, or in the course of, my employment (ss 3, 9)`
-- Ashore on shore leave in Melbourne, not at home: an injury (s 9(2)(b)(ii)); on board, climbing the mast for a bet: no.
#ASSERT `Is it an injury under the Seafarers Act` `a physical or mental injury, other than a disease, or an aggravation of one` FALSE `ashore on an ordinary break from the ship, not at home` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: it is an "injury" under s 3`
#ASSERT `Is it an injury under the Seafarers Act` `a physical or mental injury, other than a disease, or an aggravation of one` FALSE `on board the ship I was employed on` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: it did not arise out of, or in the course of, my employment (ss 3, 9)`
-- Noise-induced hearing loss to which her employment contributed in a material degree: an injury; if not: no.
#ASSERT `Is it an injury under the Seafarers Act` `an ailment or disease, or its aggravation` TRUE `somewhere else` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: it is an "injury" under s 3`
#ASSERT `Is it an injury under the Seafarers Act` `an ailment or disease, or its aggravation` FALSE `somewhere else` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: a disease must have been contributed to in a material degree by my employment (s 3)`
#ASSERT `Is it an injury under the Seafarers Act` `an ailment or disease, or its aggravation` TRUE `somewhere else` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: it resulted from reasonable disciplinary action, or a failure to get a promotion, transfer or benefit (s 3)`
#ASSERT `Is it an injury under the Seafarers Act` `a physical or mental injury, other than a disease, or an aggravation of one` FALSE `somewhere else` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: it did not arise out of, or in the course of, my employment (ss 3, 9)`

§§ `s 26`

#ASSERT `Is compensation payable to me` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: compensation is payable for the injury (s 26(1)), and for reasonable medical treatment (s 28)`
#ASSERT `Is compensation payable to me` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `only medical expenses: the injury has not resulted in death, incapacity for work or impairment (ss 26(1), 28(2))`
#ASSERT `Is compensation payable to me` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: an intentionally self-inflicted injury is not compensated (s 26(2))`
#ASSERT `Is compensation payable to me` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: an injury caused by my serious and wilful misconduct is not compensated unless it results in death or serious and permanent impairment (s 26(3))`

§§ `ss 62, 63`

-- She told the master in writing that day, and claimed with a certificate: a claim. Without the certificate: not yet.
#ASSERT `Have I made a claim that counts under the Seafarers Act` TRUE FALSE TRUE `weekly incapacity, permanent impairment or another kind of compensation` TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `Have I made a claim that counts under the Seafarers Act` TRUE FALSE TRUE `weekly incapacity, permanent impairment or another kind of compensation` FALSE
#ASSERT `Have I made a claim that counts under the Seafarers Act` TRUE FALSE TRUE `medical expenses, compensation on a death, or funeral costs (ss 28 to 30)` FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `Have I made a claim that counts under the Seafarers Act` FALSE FALSE TRUE `weekly incapacity, permanent impairment or another kind of compensation` TRUE

§§ `ss 72, 73, 73A`

-- Her incapacity claim reaches the company on 2 March 2026: decided by 14 March 2026, or taken to be disallowed (s 73).
#ASSERT `By when must my employer decide my claim` `incapacity for work, property, or the cost of medical treatment (s 73)` (Date 2 3 2026) FALSE (Date 1 1 2026) (Date 1 1 2026) FALSE (Date 1 1 2026) EQUALS Date 14 3 2026
-- It asks for her shoulder scans on 5 March and gets them on 20 March: 12 days after that, 1 April 2026.
#ASSERT `By when must my employer decide my claim` `incapacity for work, property, or the cost of medical treatment (s 73)` (Date 2 3 2026) TRUE (Date 5 3 2026) (Date 20 3 2026) FALSE (Date 1 1 2026) EQUALS Date 1 4 2026
-- The Authority allows a further period to 30 April 2026.
#ASSERT `By when must my employer decide my claim` `incapacity for work, property, or the cost of medical treatment (s 73)` (Date 2 3 2026) FALSE (Date 1 1 2026) (Date 1 1 2026) TRUE (Date 30 4 2026) EQUALS Date 30 4 2026
-- A dependant's death claim received 2 March 2026: 60 days, to 1 May 2026; with a request on 10 March answered on
-- 20 March, 9 more days (11 to 19 March), to 10 May 2026 (s 72).
#ASSERT `By when must my employer decide my claim` `compensation on a death (s 72)` (Date 2 3 2026) FALSE (Date 1 1 2026) (Date 1 1 2026) FALSE (Date 1 1 2026) EQUALS Date 1 5 2026
#ASSERT `By when must my employer decide my claim` `compensation on a death (s 72)` (Date 2 3 2026) TRUE (Date 10 3 2026) (Date 20 3 2026) FALSE (Date 1 1 2026) EQUALS Date 10 5 2026
-- A permanent impairment claim received 2 March 2026: 30 days, to 1 April 2026 (s 73A).
#ASSERT `By when must my employer decide my claim` `permanent impairment (s 73A)` (Date 2 3 2026) FALSE (Date 1 1 2026) (Date 1 1 2026) FALSE (Date 1 1 2026) EQUALS Date 1 4 2026

§§ `s 28`

#ASSERT `Can I claim travel to treatment under the Seafarers Act` TRUE TRUE TRUE 0
#ASSERT NOT `Can I claim travel to treatment under the Seafarers Act` TRUE TRUE FALSE 0
#ASSERT `Can I claim travel to treatment under the Seafarers Act` TRUE FALSE FALSE 60
#ASSERT NOT `Can I claim travel to treatment under the Seafarers Act` TRUE FALSE FALSE 50

§§ `ss 31, 38`

-- The arguments: incapacitated; earlier weeks; NWE; suitable earnings; hours employed; normal hours; worked as a seafarer;
-- AWOTE; the three minimum earnings figures; a dependent prescribed person; dependent prescribed children; pension age;
-- injured within a year of it; weeks already compensated; imprisoned.
-- Week 11 ashore after being returned to her home port: one of the first 45 weeks (the formula is not in the deposit).
#ASSERT `How much weekly compensation this week` TRUE 10 2000 0 0 38 FALSE 2000 254.46 62.99 31.50 TRUE 2 FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE EQUALS `one of the first 45 weeks: compensation by the s 31(2) formula, which is not in the deposited text`
-- Week 51, not working: 75% of $2,000 = $1,500, above the minimum earnings of the lesser of $254.46 + $62.99 + 2 x $31.50
-- = $380.45 and 90% x $2,000 = $1,800 (s 31(5)(a), (8), (9)).
#ASSERT `How much weekly compensation this week` TRUE 50 2000 0 0 38 FALSE 2000 254.46 62.99 31.50 TRUE 2 FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE EQUALS `an amount per week after the first 45 weeks (s 31(4), (5))` 1500
-- Back ashore on 19 hours (50%) earning $800: 85% of $2,000 less $800 = $900 (s 31(5)(c)).
#ASSERT `How much weekly compensation this week` TRUE 50 2000 800 19 38 FALSE 2000 254.46 62.99 31.50 TRUE 2 FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE EQUALS `an amount per week after the first 45 weeks (s 31(4), (5))` 900
-- Back at sea for part of a week, 10 hours, earning $1,200: treated as 100% (s 31(6)): $2,000 less $1,200 = $800.
#ASSERT `How much weekly compensation this week` TRUE 50 2000 1200 10 38 TRUE 2000 254.46 62.99 31.50 TRUE 2 FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE EQUALS `an amount per week after the first 45 weeks (s 31(4), (5))` 800
-- A master on $5,000 a week, not working: 75% is $3,750, cut to 150% of AWOTE, $3,000 (s 31(7)).
#ASSERT `How much weekly compensation this week` TRUE 50 5000 0 0 38 FALSE 2000 254.46 62.99 31.50 FALSE 0 FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE EQUALS `an amount per week after the first 45 weeks (s 31(4), (5))` 3000
-- A trainee on $400 a week, not employed, able to earn $100: $300 - $100 = $200, raised to the minimum earnings, the lesser
-- of $380.45 and 90% x $400 = $360: $360 (s 31(8), (9)).
#ASSERT `How much weekly compensation this week` TRUE 50 400 100 0 38 FALSE 2000 254.46 62.99 31.50 TRUE 2 FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE EQUALS `an amount per week after the first 45 weeks (s 31(4), (5))` 360
-- The same trainee with no dependants: the minimum is $254.46, below $360: $254.46.
#ASSERT `How much weekly compensation this week` TRUE 50 400 100 0 38 FALSE 2000 254.46 62.99 31.50 FALSE 0 FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE EQUALS `an amount per week after the first 45 weeks (s 31(4), (5))` 254.46
-- Stops: at pension age; after 52 weeks for an injury within the year before it; in prison; when no longer incapacitated.
#ASSERT `How much weekly compensation this week` TRUE 50 2000 0 0 38 FALSE 2000 254.46 62.99 31.50 TRUE 2 TRUE FALSE 0 FALSE EQUALS `none: I have reached pension age (s 38(1))`
#ASSERT `How much weekly compensation this week` TRUE 52 2000 0 0 38 FALSE 2000 254.46 62.99 31.50 TRUE 2 TRUE TRUE 52 FALSE EQUALS `none: the 52 weeks for an injury suffered within a year before pension age are used up (s 38(2))`
#ASSERT `How much weekly compensation this week` TRUE 50 2000 0 0 38 FALSE 2000 254.46 62.99 31.50 TRUE 2 FALSE FALSE 0 TRUE EQUALS `none: I am imprisoned for an offence (s 38(3))`
#ASSERT `How much weekly compensation this week` FALSE 50 2000 0 0 38 FALSE 2000 254.46 62.99 31.50 TRUE 2 FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE EQUALS `none: I am not incapacitated for work this week`

§§ `ss 39, 40`

-- 12% of the printed $100,778.56 maximum: $12,093.4272. At 9%: nothing. The loss of a finger at 5%: $5,038.928.
#ASSERT `How much permanent impairment compensation under the Seafarers Act` FALSE 12 100778.56 EQUALS 12093.4272
#ASSERT `How much permanent impairment compensation under the Seafarers Act` FALSE 9 100778.56 EQUALS 0
#ASSERT `How much permanent impairment compensation under the Seafarers Act` TRUE 5 100778.56 EQUALS 5038.928

§§ `s 29`

#ASSERT `What lump sum is payable on a seafarer's death` TRUE TRUE 412000 EQUALS `the full lump sum, for all the dependants, shared as the employer determines (s 29(3), (8))` 412000
#ASSERT `What lump sum is payable on a seafarer's death` FALSE TRUE 412000 EQUALS `an appropriate amount, up to this, for the partly dependent dependants (s 29(4))` 412000
#ASSERT `What lump sum is payable on a seafarer's death` FALSE FALSE 412000 EQUALS `no lump sum: the employee left no dependants (s 29(2)); medical and funeral costs are still payable (ss 28, 30)`

§§ `ss 78, 88`

#ASSERT `By when must I ask my employer to reconsider` (Date 10 2 2026) EQUALS Date 12 3 2026
#ASSERT `Can I go to the ART` FALSE EQUALS `first ask my employer in writing to reconsider it, within 30 days (s 78); only the decision on reconsideration can go to the ART (ss 76, 88)`
#ASSERT `Can I go to the ART` TRUE EQUALS `I may apply to the ART for review of the reconsideration decision (s 88(1))`
